---
<Prose title="Make Jotra the app for .md and .txt" description="Register Jotra as the default app for Markdown and plain text on macOS, Windows and Linux, so a double-click opens the file in Jotra." path="/guides/default-md-app/" lead="Once per file type. After that a double-click in Finder or Explorer opens the file in Jotra.">
  <h2>macOS</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open Settings (<kbd class="kc">⌘</kbd><kbd class="kc">,</kbd>), Integrations. Open these file types with Jotra has a box per group: Plain text &amp; logs, Markdown, Config &amp; data, Source code. Tick the groups you want; a ticked box stays ticked while Jotra holds the type.</li>
    <li>macOS keeps the choice per type, so claiming Markdown does not change what opens <code>.txt</code>. To hand a type to another app, pick that app in Finder: Get Info on one file, Open with, Change All.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Windows</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Right-click any <code>.md</code> file, Open with, Choose another app.</li>
    <li>Pick Jotra and tick "Always use this app".</li>
    <li>Repeat for <code>.txt</code> or any other extension you want here.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Linux</h2>
  <p>The .deb installs a desktop entry named <code>Jotra.desktop</code>, so Jotra appears in your file manager's Open With list. To make it the default from a terminal:</p>
  <pre><code>xdg-mime default Jotra.desktop text/markdown
xdg-mime default Jotra.desktop text/plain</code></pre>
  <p>With the AppImage, register it first with AppImageLauncher (or any integrator that writes a desktop entry), then run the same commands with the name it created.</p>
  <h2>What opening a file means</h2>
  <p>The file opens in a tab under its own name and is saved back to its own path as you edit. If the file changes on disk while it has unsaved edits, Jotra asks before replacing them. Closing the tab saves your last edits and never deletes the file.</p>
  <p>Related: <a href={`${base}/docs/default-app/`}>the default app in the docs</a>.</p>
</Prose>
